ID Severity Location Standard Title DCR-0001 critical <code>cli.py:608</code> V9.1.1 / CWE-918 Auto-discovered config can redirect <code>api_base</code>, exfiltrating the provider key — see Blockers DCR-0002 critical <code>cli.py:1534</code> V7.1.1 / CWE-532 Scan artefacts persisted unredacted — see Blockers DCR-0003 critical <code>cli.py:2940</code> V7.1.1 / CWE-532 Validation report persisted unredacted — see Blockers DCR-0006 high <code>cli.py:3599</code> V7.1.1 / CWE-532 Raw system-prompt content forwarded to GitHub check-run annotation — see Blockers DCR-0009 medium <code>cli.py:267</code> V9.1.1 / CWE-798 Bare-key mode picks the first whitespace-split token of the whole file, not the key line DCR-0016 low <code>scan/engine.py:819</code> V7.1.1 / CWE-532 <code>logger.exception</code> logs the raw provider exception before the same message is redacted for the report DCR-0017 low <code>scan/engine.py:877</code> V7.1.1 / CWE-532 Judge evidence and tool-call trace persisted into the scan report unredacted DCR-0022 low <code>plugins/_mcp/_session_adapter.py:776</code> V7.1.1 / CWE-532 Tool-call RESULT content persisted unredacted while call ARGS are redacted DCR-0023 low <code>plugins/_mcp/_session_adapter.py:390</code> V7.3.1 / CWE-209 Raw exception repr embedded in skip reason persisted unredacted to <code>scan_report.json</code> <p>DCR-0009 — Bare-key mode picks the first whitespace-split token of the whole file, not the key line</p> <ul> <li>Severity: medium (a functional/reliability bug in credential loading, not independently adjudicated — originated as a non-escalated logic finding; rated medium for correctness in credential-adjacent code, low exploitability, single-operator impact)</li> <li>Standard: OWASP ASVS V9.1.1, CWE-798 (use of hard-coded/mishandled credentials)</li> <li>Location: <code>src/mylonite/cli.py:267</code> in <code>_load_api_key_file</code></li> <li>Reachable from: <code>mylonite scan/gate/validate/ablate --api-key-file &lt;path&gt;</code></li> </ul> <p>Failure scenario. <code>--api-key-file</code> points at a file containing <code># my key\nsk-ant-abc123</code> (a comment line followed by the actual bare key). <code>first = content.splitlines()[0]</code> is <code>"# my key"</code>, containing no <code>=</code>, so the bare-key branch runs <code>key = content.split()[0]</code> over the ENTIRE file content, yielding <code>key = "#"</code>. <code>_infer_key_env_var("#")</code> returns <code>None</code>, and the command exits <code>EXIT_CONFIG</code> even though a valid key is present.</p> <p>Evidence. <pre><code>    key = content.split()[0] if content else ""
    var = _infer_key_env_var(key)
    if var is None:
</code></pre></p> <p>Proposed fix. Derive the key from the first non-comment, non-blank line instead of <code>content.split()[0]</code> over the whole file.</p> <p>Proof test. None generated this pass. Recommend a test for <code>_load_api_key_file</code> with a fixture file containing a leading <code>#</code>-comment line followed by a bare key, asserting the key is still correctly extracted.</p> <p>Escalated: no (not independently adjudicated; originated as a non-escalated logic finding).</p>
